ere very excited  but anxious not because we didn't love  Dragon Ball or the idea of it coming  back but for a lot of us we didn't know  if it would feel right there's something  about the original series that feels  sort of distinct and it's clear that  whatever that dragon ball Ness is people  find it difficult to replicate  faithfully without turning a large  portion of the fanbase sour there is  something about Akira Toriyama's  characters from how they are designed to  how they act and conduct themselves that  oozes personality and even hints  oftentimes at hidden complexity from its  long list of villains and heroes Akira  Toriyama created a world of his own and  brought to life by filling it with tons  of colorful interesting and endearing  characters pushing the main message of  Dragon Ball which is to push your own  limits pass what others think is  possible despite great adversity it's  not easy to create a character and I  think especially when it comes to Dragon  Ball Z it's easy to introduce or create  a character that takes themselves way  too seriously and I think that's really  evident if you look at most of the  o'seas created by the fanbase and so  maybe it was a good thing that Dragon  Ball ended the way it did it left the  series open-ended with the journey  continuing indefinitely as Goku went on  fighting and training the future  successor as the Earth's protector  Dragon Ball Z had already created a  massive problem for the series in  focusing on giant climactic battles that  continuously increased the scale and  consequences of each subsequent  iteration by the end of the series many  of us became desensitized to the stakes  and the consequences because they had  become too impossibly large to fully  appreciate in a lot of ways but in 2012  your  Toriyama decided to continue Dragon  Balls story and had a remedy for that  very problem from the very first scene I  was introduced to this brand-new  character I immediately fell in love  with him 